            summary: "Commit authors, hooks, locks, upstreams, network failures, and rejected writes.", symbol: "wrench.and.screwdriver", keywords: ["git error", "commit failed", "push rejected", "pull failed", "fetch failed", "index lock", "permission denied", "authentication", "timeout", "identity", "commit author", "user name", "user email", "author name", "author email"], sections: [
                HelpSection("Git cannot run or commit", ["Check the Git executable in Settings → Developer Tools, then Apply Workflow Settings. RepoDeck uses the installed Git configuration, including commit author settings, credential helpers, SSH setup, and hooks.", "A hook failure needs the hook's reported issue fixed. Hooks and credential helpers that need interactive input may require an external terminal."]),
                HelpSection("Configure your commit author in RepoDeck", ["Select a repository and choose Configure Author under Commit author in the sidebar footer. When Git resolves a complete author, the action is Edit Author. The same setup is available in Repository Settings and from commit-author errors. Unavailable under Commit author means Git could not resolve the current author; Retry reads it again, and Configure Author opens the settings and full diagnostic.", "Enter your default name and email. The form edits Git’s user.name and user.email settings. Choose This repository to save them for that repository, including linked worktrees sharing its configuration, or My Git default to set the defaults used by Git on this Mac and other Git apps. Repository, worktree, author.name/author.email, or environment settings can override those defaults.", "Review the displayed destination and choose Save Commit Author. Author for new commits shows the author Git resolves for ordinary new commits, which may differ from the defaults you saved. Opening the form does not change settings. A failed save preserves your entries; because Git writes the name and email separately, an interrupted save may have saved one field. Read the error and reload the settings before saving again.", "If the defaults can be read but the author is unavailable, you can still save the defaults to repair them. The warning retains Git’s diagnostic and offers Reload Author. If an author-specific or environment override remains invalid, correct that override at its source and reload. A successfully saved default does not guarantee that every overriding setting is valid.", "SSH keys authenticate access to a remote, and your GitHub or GitLab account identifies the hosting account. They do not automatically supply the author stored inside commits. The sidebar’s GitHub account row is separate from Commit author. Review the displayed author, staged changes, and commit message before choosing Commit. Existing commits retain their authors, and cherry-pick and rebase normally preserve the original commit’s author."]),
                HelpSection("Set your default name and email for this repository", ["As a terminal alternative, select the repository and choose Open in Terminal. Run these commands from its directory, replacing Your Name and you@example.com with the default name and email you want recorded in new commits. Keep the quotation marks around your values.", "git config --local user.name \"Your Name\"\ngit config --local user.email \"you@example.com\"", "These defaults apply to this repository, including linked worktrees that share its configuration. They leave your global defaults unchanged. Ask Git which author it resolves before committing:", "git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT", "The output includes the author’s name, email, timestamp, and time zone. Reading user.name and user.email alone does not account for author-specific or environment overrides. Inspect their origins with git config --show-origin --get user.name, git config --show-origin --get user.email, git config --show-origin --get author.name, and git config --show-origin --get author.email. GIT_AUTHOR_NAME and GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL can also take precedence. Use the Git executable configured in RepoDeck; your terminal environment may differ from the app’s.", "Return to RepoDeck, refresh, and review its displayed commit author, staged changes, and commit message. Choose Commit again only when these are correct. These commands do not rewrite existing commits."]),
                HelpSection("Push rejected or no upstream", ["Fetch and inspect incoming commits first. Set the intended tracking branch in Branches & Worktrees if no upstream exists. Integrate changes through a reviewed pull, merge, or rebase workflow before pushing again. Do not use force push as a generic fix for someone else's newer commits.", "Auto-Rebase on Rejected Push is optional and may require conflict or autostash recovery. Read the error and inspect Conflicts and Stashes rather than repeating it blindly."]),
                HelpSection("Lock or operation already in progress", ["Another Git process may own an index lock or be performing a merge/rebase. Wait for it to finish and inspect the repository in a terminal. Do not delete a lock file while a Git process may still be using it. Use the existing operation's Continue/Abort workflow instead of starting a competing one."]),
                HelpSection("Network, authentication, or timeout", ["Check connectivity, the remote URL, and the credentials/SSH configuration used by your Git executable. Use an external terminal if the tool needs a prompt. Git's own remote authentication is separate from the gh/glab Reviews connection.", "A timeout does not by itself prove the remote made no change. Fetch and inspect remote state before retrying a push. Preserve any local error details and unsent message while you determine what completed."])
            ], related: [.syncAndUndo, .conflicts, .settings, .reviewTroubleshooting]),
        HelpArticle(id: .installation, category: .start, title: "Install, update, and open RepoDeck",
            summary: "macOS requirements, the disk image, and first-launch approval.", symbol: "square.and.arrow.down", keywords: ["install", "download", "update", "Gatekeeper", "unnotarized", "ad hoc", "Apple silicon", "Intel", "macOS", "Open Anyway"], sections: [
                HelpSection("Install or update", ["RepoDeck requires macOS 15 or later. The release installer includes Apple silicon and Intel support. Download the installer and its SHA-256 checksum from the project's GitHub release page, verify the download, open the disk image, and drag RepoDeck to Applications.", "To update, quit the running app before replacing it with the newer copy. Releases are available at github.com/sergio-farfan/repodeck/releases. RepoDeck does not provide an automatic in-app updater."]),
                HelpSection("First launch", ["The standard release is ad-hoc signed and not notarized. After verifying a trusted download and attempting to open it, you may need System Settings → Privacy & Security → Open Anyway. Device-management policy can restrict this option. Follow Apple's app-specific instructions; do not disable system-wide security to open an app.", "Developer ID signing and notarization are optional future distribution choices, not requirements for using this standard release."]),
                HelpSection("Build from source", ["The project uses Swift Package Manager and Swift 6.2 or newer. The documented development commands are swift build, swift test, and swift run RepoDeck. See the repository's README for packaging instructions. Local Git work requires Git; gh and glab are optional for hosting integration."])
            ], related: [.gettingStarted, .settings, .troubleshooting]),
        HelpArticle(id: .safety, category: .troubleshooting, title: "Understand safety limits and recovery",
            summary: "What previews, drafts, undo, and coordination can protect.", symbol: "shield", keywords: ["data loss", "backup", "stale", "external git", "recover", "reset", "force", "limitations"], sections: [
                HelpSection("Preview the current state", ["Repository actions are checked against the worktree, branch, commit, and operation state you reviewed. If any of those change, refresh and create a new preview. Other Git programs do not share RepoDeck's coordinator and can still race an individual Git command."]),
                HelpSection("Keep recoverable work", ["Commit or back up important local work before a significant history change. A stash does not include ignored files by default. Sync Undo is limited to its recorded branch/worktree/commit, does not undo a remote push, and is not a general backup.", "Save and Mark Resolved are distinct. Read a whole-file staging or Abort confirmation carefully. Safe worktree deletion refuses local files that would otherwise be lost."]),
                HelpSection("Errors and unsent text", ["Commit and review drafts are preserved on errors; successful submissions clear only the text that was actually submitted. Before reloading a conflict document after an external change, copy any intended result you need to keep.", "For uncertain remote creation/comments, preserve the pending action and let the app reconcile it when the service is available. If you need manual recovery, inspect Git and the hosting service first rather than assuming the first attempt did nothing."]),
                HelpSection("Use the appropriate tool", ["Use an external terminal/editor for unsupported encodings, complex conflicts, interactive rebase, terminal prompts, and advanced Git operations. Use a hosting service's browser UI for inline threaded reviews and unsupported provider features. RepoDeck's previews do not replace reviewing the actual content you are about to commit, stage, or publish."])
            ], related: [.conflicts, .syncAndUndo, .stashes, .troubleshooting])
    ]
